# Editors and pagers, each opened on a real file.

shot    elvrec
for     elvrec elvprsv vi.elvis elvis_input
cap     elvis's crash-recovery pair: elvprsv preserves an interrupted
cap     session, elvrec brings it back.  Nothing has been preserved on this
cap     disk, so elvrec lists nothing at all and only names the file when
cap     you ask for one by name.  `exrecover' is the same job for the other
cap     vi here, and says what it wants.
run     elvrec
wait    6
run     elvrec /dd/tmp/none
wait    6
run     exrecover
wait    6
run     elvprsv /dd/tmp/none
wait    6

shot    emacs
for     emacs emacs.mm1 umacs
cap     MicroEmacs 4.00 editing a file from the disk, with its own mode
cap     line at the foot.
run     emacs /dd/SYS/motd
wait    10
quit    \030\003
run     emacs.mm1 < /nil
wait    10

shot    hexed
cap     This hex editor cannot start: it writes its work file to /r0, the
cap     RAM disk os9exec has no way to provide, and says so.  Twenty
cap     programs here reach for /r0; DOC/STATUS lists them.  `beav' is the
cap     binary editor that needs nothing, and `hexedit' -- a different
cap     program with a similar name -- works once TERMCAP is set as a
cap     capability string.
run     hexed /dd/CMDS/today
wait    6
quit    q

shot    hexedit
cap     HEXPERT V2.4, viewing a module.  Like gnuchess it reads TERMCAP as
cap     the capability string itself, so `. /dd/SYS/termcap.entry' comes
cap     first; without it the bare form prints the terminal type and exits.
cap     One 16-byte row and the mode is the whole display -- that is the
cap     program, not a clipped capture; arrows, `n' and space all leave
cap     it where it is.  Its -d option is separately broken -- `file not
cap     accessible' for a file that is readable, which this disproves.
run     . /dd/SYS/termcap.entry
wait    4
run     hexedit /dd/CMDS/today
wait    12
kill

shot    pbyte
for     pbyte chbase
cap     pbyte patches bytes in a file at a hex offset -- the smallest binary
cap     editor here.  Three bytes at offset 0 turn `Wel' into `ABC'.
cap     `chbase' beside it is NOT a module-base changer whatever DOC/INDEX
cap     said: it converts a NUMBER from one base to another.  Both used to
cap     be captured with no arguments, which showed their usage lines and
cap     nothing either program can do.
run     cat /dd/SYS/motd > /dd/tmp/pb
wait    5
run     cat /dd/tmp/pb
wait    5
run     pbyte /dd/tmp/pb 0 41 42 43
wait    6
run     cat /dd/tmp/pb
wait    5
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/pb
wait    4
run     chbase 255 10 16
wait    8

shot    sed
for     sed sed_1.06 psc
cap     sed doing what a stream editor does, twice over, on the disk's own
cap     documentation.
run     head -n 8 /dd/SYS/holidays > /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    6
run     sed "s/Day/DAY/g" /dd/tmp/hol.txt
wait    8
run     psc < /nil
wait    8

shot    vi
for     vi view
cap     The EFFO vi -- its source in SRC/effo_vi is the Berkeley ex source
cap     itself, not a clone, which is why it has the name and keeps it.
cap     WHAT YOU SEE HERE IS NOT WHAT ITS USERS SEE.  Under this harness --
cap     a pseudo-terminal rendered as vt100 -- it paints the first line and
cap     a half-drawn rule and no more; on a real terminal it works fine and
cap     is used daily.  Tried and did NOT explain it: 24 lines and 36,
cap     TERMCAP as a path and as an entry, ^L and ^R.  So this is a fact
cap     about the harness until somebody explains it.  `elvis', `vi_nocio'
cap     and REBUILT/vi_1.0 fill the screen under both, each with its own
cap     card.
run     vi /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    20
send    \033
wait    3
send    1G
wait    10
quit    \033:q!\r
run     view < /nil
wait    10

shot    elvis
for     elvis vi.elvis elvis_input ctags.elvis
cap     elvis, Steve Kirkendall's vi -- the one on this disk that paints a
cap     full screen.  Opened on the collection's own README-CIO, twenty-two
cap     lines of it at once, exactly as vi should.  `vi.elvis',
cap     `elvis_input' and `ctags.elvis' travel with it -- the same editor
cap     under the name a Unix hand types, its input helper, and its tags
cap     builder. they are asked for after elvis has been left.
run     elvis /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r
run     ctags.elvis /dd/SRC/keep/about.c
wait    10
run     ls -l /dd/tags
wait    8
run     elvis_input /dd/tmp/ei
wait    8
run     vi.elvis -?
wait    10

shot    pvic
for     vi_1.0
cap     PVIC 1.0, the Portable VI Clone, in CMDS/REBUILT because the name
cap     `vi' was already taken by the EFFO one; `vi_cio' and `vi_nocio' are
cap     the 1.0a builds of the same program.  It is a working vi: tildes down
cap     the left, the file painted a screenful at a time, and o-i-escape-:wq
cap     writes the line you typed.  Asked for by path, since it is not on
cap     the login PATH.
run     /dd/CMDS/REBUILT/vi_1.0 /dd/DOC/README-CIO
wait    18
quit    \033:q!\r

